/* ===== Cam's voice: the animalese letter library =====
   One 8-bit unsigned, mono, 44.1 kHz PCM blob, base64'd so a classic
   <script> can carry it: Tweego emits src/js as classic scripts, and
   fetch/XHR are blocked on file://, so the audio cannot be a file next to
   story.html. voice.js decodes it into one AudioBuffer at first use.

   26 slices of 0.075 s (3308 samples each), letters A..Z in order, with no
   header: slice n starts at n * 3308. Spaces, punctuation and digits are
   silence in the original library, so only A..Z are ever asked for.

   The source recording is 0.15 s per letter; the library plays only the
   first 0.075 s of each slice (source.start(offset, 0.075 * pitch), with
   pitch <= 1), so the rest was trimmed off here. That halved the payload
   (172 KB -> 86 KB) without removing a sample the player can hear.

   Source: github.com/Acedio/animalese.js — animalese.wav (2014), which
   jeff-hykin/animalese ships byte-identical as a 7-bit-packed JS string.
   Header stripped and re-encoded here.

   LICENCE: the samples are CC BY 4.0, attribution to Josh Simmons required;
   the wrapper code is MIT. See docs/adr/0002-cam-speaks-aloud.md. */
window.VoiceLetters = window.VoiceLetters || {};
window.VoiceLetters.sampleRate = 44100;
window.VoiceLetters.secondsPerLetter = 0.075;
window.VoiceLetters.pcmBase64 =
  "";
